"Monitors single sequential measurements with control limits estimated from a stable process baseline, often paired with a moving-range panel.\n\nUse when: Measurements arrive one at a time rather than in rational subgroups. A stable baseline can support monitoring for unusual shifts or variation.\n\nAvoid when: Strong autocorrelation, changing measurement definitions, or an unstable baseline invalidate the chosen limits.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal position encodes observation order or time. Vertical position encodes individual values; a companion panel encodes successive absolute differences.\n\nBuild: Choose and justify the stable baseline period. Estimate the center and within-process variation using the stated moving-range method. Draw individual and moving-range panels with labeled control limits and documented signal rules.\n\nCheck: Check limits use the correct moving-range constants. Verify baseline limits are frozen for subsequent monitoring unless a justified process change occurs. Distinguish control limits from specification or acceptance limits.\n\nPitfalls: Recalculating limits after every unusual point can hide meaningful process shifts. A signal warrants investigation rather than automatic removal or proof of a specific cause.\n\nExample: Monitor synthetic daily fill measurements with an individual-value panel and a two-observation moving-range panel derived from the same baseline.\n\nAlternatives: X-bar and R chart, CUSUM chart, EWMA chart."
